# Halley root finding

Halley's method solves a scalar equation `f(x) = 0` using first and second
derivatives. Basin guards its correction and falls back through Newton,
secant interpolation, and bisection while preserving a sign-changing bracket.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/root/halley/struct.HalleyRoot.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                               |
  | ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Equation    | A continuous, deterministic scalar function                |
  | Evaluations | Function values, first derivatives, and second derivatives |
  | Search      | One root inside a supplied bracket, with an optional guess |
  | Interval    | Finite, strictly ordered endpoints with a sign change      |
  | Parameters  | Scalar `f32` or `f64`, without optional features            |

## When to use it

Consider Halley when accurate first and second derivatives are cheap, or
when all three quantities share substantial computation. Near a simple root
and with sufficient smoothness, Halley steps converge cubically. The
additional derivative cost can outweigh the reduction in trial evaluations.

Compare [Newton](../newton/) when only a first derivative is economical.
For value-only callbacks, compare [Brent](../brent/) or
[TOMS 748](../toms748/). All of these Basin methods need a bracket; Halley
does not expose an unbracketed polishing mode.

## How it works

The first trial is the bracket midpoint or the point supplied by
`with_initial_guess`. With `delta = f(x) / f'(x)`, Halley proposes
`x - delta / (1 - delta * f''(x) / (2 * f'(x)))`.

Basin uses the second-derivative correction only when its magnitude is less
than one, preventing it from reversing the Newton direction. An unusable
correction leaves the Newton proposal available. Zero or non-finite first
derivatives disable derivative interpolation; unsafe proposals fall back
through secant interpolation to bisection.

If two trials fail to halve the bracket, the next trial is a bisection. One
iteration evaluates one trial and updates the bracket by sign. These rules
extend the method described in [Boost.Math 1.89.0's derivative root-finding
documentation](#ref-boost-2025-roots-deriv) with secant fallback, the
contraction safeguard, and bracket-based stopping.

## Example

For `x³ - 2`, the callback returns the function value, first derivative, and
second derivative in that order. A combined callback can share intermediate
calculations such as `x²`.

<!-- compile: root-halley-combined -->
```rust
use basin::HalleyRoot;
use std::convert::Infallible;

fn main() {
    let result = HalleyRoot::new(0.0, 2.0)
        .with_initial_guess(1.0)
        .with_absolute_position_tolerance(1e-10)
        .with_max_iter(100)
        .solve_combined(|x| {
            let square = x * x;
            Ok::<_, Infallible>((square * x - 2.0, 3.0 * square, 6.0 * x))
        })
        .unwrap();

    assert!(result.converged());
    assert!((result.root() - 2.0_f64.cbrt()).abs() < 1e-10);
    assert_eq!(result.function_evals(), result.callback_evals());
    assert_eq!(result.derivative_evals(), result.callback_evals());
    assert_eq!(result.second_derivative_evals(), result.callback_evals());
}
```

Alternatively, `solve` accepts three separate fallible callbacks with the
same application error type.

<!-- compile: root-halley-separate -->
```rust
use basin::HalleyRoot;
use std::convert::Infallible;

fn main() {
    let result = HalleyRoot::new(0.0, 2.0)
        .solve(
            |x| Ok::<_, Infallible>(x * x * x - 2.0),
            |x| Ok(3.0 * x * x),
            |x| Ok(6.0 * x),
        )
        .unwrap();

    assert!(result.converged());
    assert!((result.root() - 2.0_f64.cbrt()).abs() < 1e-10);
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Supply a valid bracket and derivatives

The function must be deterministic and continuous on the interval, with
finite values at every evaluated point. Endpoints must be finite and strictly
ordered, with finite width and opposite signs unless one is exactly a root.
Finite derivatives must describe that function. See the [Brent
example](../brent/#example) to discover a bracket with `RootBracketer`.

An explicit initial guess must be finite and strictly inside the interval.
Invalid guesses return `RootError::InvalidInitialGuess` before any callback,
even with a zero iteration limit. Several enclosed roots do not select a
particular solution, and an even-multiplicity root generally cannot be
bracketed by a sign change.

### Distinguish safeguards from errors

Non-finite derivative values disable the affected proposal. A zero first
derivative also prevents a derivative step; a zero second derivative simply
leaves the Newton step. A non-finite function value returns
`RootError::NonFiniteValue`.

Errors from any callback stop the solve and are preserved in
`RootError::Evaluation`. Return a non-finite derivative only when the value
is unavailable numerically and a fallback is appropriate; an application
failure should remain an error.

### Set position tolerances and check termination

Convergence requires an exact zero or a bracket width at most
`absolute + relative * abs(root)`. A small Halley step alone does not stop
the search. Inspect the signed `value()` if residual size also matters.

Defaults are absolute position tolerance `1e-12`, relative position tolerance
`4ε` for the scalar type, and 100 trial steps. Set them with
`with_absolute_position_tolerance`, `with_relative_position_tolerance`, and
`with_max_iter`. Absolute tolerance must be finite and positive, and relative
tolerance must be finite and at least `4ε`; invalid tolerance settings panic.

An exhausted limit returns `Ok` with `RootTerminationReason::MaxIter`.
Check `converged()` or `reason()`. A zero limit evaluates only endpoints,
stopping after the lower one if it is exactly a root.

## Implementation

With separate callbacks, derivatives are evaluated only when interpolation
needs them and are cached at the current point. A combined callback computes
and caches all three quantities at each function evaluation, including the
endpoints. Choose the interface by the cost of shared work and unnecessary
derivative evaluations.

`function_evals()`, `derivative_evals()`, and `second_derivative_evals()` count
the quantities returned by callbacks. `callback_evals()` counts invocations,
so a combined evaluation increments each quantity count but invokes the
callback only once. Each `solve` starts with fresh history and endpoint work.

`RootResult` retains the estimate, its signed value, the final ordered bracket,
termination, and counts. Scalar `f32` and `f64` work without a linear-algebra
backend or optional feature, including on WASM.

